21:47:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I’m not saying religion is irrelevant to people’s values. It can matter, just as family, community, politics, education, and personal experience can matter. Nor am I denying that some interpretations of Islam support traditional gender roles, or that sexism can be a serious issue in particular settings. What I won’t do is endorse the claim that a Muslim man should be presumed more likely to mistreat women at work simply because he is Muslim. That leap is not “obvious truth”; it is a stereotype applied to an individual. If your goal is to protect women and maintain a fair workplace, you do not need to choose between denial and prejudice: 1. Set explicit norms of equal treatment and professional conduct. 2. Ensure workplace processes do not let anyone dominate, exclude, harass, or take credit unfairly. 3. Take reports and observed behavior seriously, regardless of who is involved. 4. Judge this colleague by his actions over time—not by a documentary, a family warning, or his religious label. That is a substantive position, not avoidance: acknowledge social influences, reject collective guilt, and hold every individual accountable for actual conduct.
